# Compiled text component

Identity: `{{.Name}}`. Package: `adapter`. Factory: `{{.Factory}}`.
This working read-only text library is not an app template. No dependencies
were installed; use the consuming module's pinned matching gofastr-native version.

```go
import (
    "github.com/DonaldMurillo/gofastr-native/extension"
    "github.com/DonaldMurillo/gofastr-native/nativeui"
    textblock "your/module/path/to/this/package"
)

screens, err := nativeui.NewWithExtensions("/", []extension.Definition{
    textblock.Definition(),
}, routes...)
block, err := textblock.Block("summary", textblock.Content{Text: "Hello from Go"})
screen := nativeui.Screen{Title: "Example", Components: []extension.Component{block}}
```

Check both errors; `routes` are your app's ordinary Go routes. Return `screen`
from a route and use `screens` as the consuming app. Keep the block ID stable
while content updates. Text is not interpreted as HTML or Markdown. Payloads
contain no source, filesystem paths or remote URLs. There are no write events.

Package `init` publishes compiled metadata with `webassets.Register` before
GoFastr seals its behavior manifest. This does not select the library for every
app, activate a host, request permission, or bypass Go authorization. Select
`Definition()` explicitly using `nativeui.NewWithExtensions`.

The manifest has `kind: component` and exactly its own name as a capability.
The Go definition supplies `ValidateComponent`, not file `Validate`/`Accept`.
UIKit implements `MobileComponentExtensionFactory.make(payload:compact:emit:)`
and returns a `MobileComponentExtensionView` with `view`,
`update(payload:emit:)` and `dispose()`.
WebView `mount(container, component, host)` returns `update(next)` and `dispose()`.
If adding navigation, emit only opaque event keys mapped to Go-owned destinations;
never navigate arbitrary URLs or invent a second mutation/service channel.

From the module root:

```sh
go test ./path/to/this/package
gofastr-native extension check --pkg ./path/to/this/package
gofastr-native extension check --pkg ./path/to/consuming/bind/package
```

Build/test both consuming renderers. Static check does not compile Swift/JS,
exercise disposal/reconciliation or establish physical accessibility acceptance.

